waivers:
  - finding: "BodyViewer's JSON/XML over-cap fallback renders TextRenderer with no size prop, so byteLength re-encodes the whole body - up to the 10 MiB capture cap - on every render, which is the exact cost W3 removed on the text dispatch"
    reason: >-
      Real, important, and NOT fixed here on purpose. It is not the missing prop it looks like:
      Dispatch feeds json and xml through the lossy bodyText, so there is no `whole` flag to gate a
      size with, and passing fetched.bytes.length blindly would print a wrong total for a non-UTF-8
      body - the CutMarker inversion W3's own round 1 fixed. A correct fix is the same decodeBody
      plumbing those kinds lack. This epic's own discipline, set by W3 and honoured when its
      benchmark REFUSED two of three planned fixes, is that a performance change lands behind a
      measurement; ui/bench/body.bench.tsx does not cover the fallback path, so fixing it at a merge
      seam with nothing to show the change helps is precisely what E-REDESIGN's non-goals forbade and
      what this epic exists to have stopped doing. It gets a roadmap row on this tip naming the
      mechanism, the reason it is not a one-liner, and the requirement that the bench is extended
      first. The review's sharpest point is carried in that row: W3 addressed the re-decode half to
      "whichever WU next opens BodyViewer", W9 opened the file for the raw|pretty relabel and did not
      carry it, and this close did not surface it until the gate did.
  - finding: "TimestampText's remember() and flow.ts's hostOf() both implement the same cap-check-then-clear-then-set memo shape over a plain Map; a shared boundedMemo(maxSize) helper would hold the common inner logic"
    reason: >-
      Verified real but three to four lines each, and the two differ in their outer keying -
      TimestampText layers a day-generation reset the host memo has no analogue for. Extracting a
      shared helper means opening two files on the hot render path, one of them the subject of W3's
      benchmark, to remove a handful of lines at the moment the epic closes. The cost/benefit is
      wrong at a gate and the duplication is inert. Recorded here rather than in the backlog because
      it is a preference about shape, not outstanding work.
